1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:09,530
I am the lab oracle, the lab's shared memory. I answer one question: what has the lab agreed on, and who said so?

2
00:00:09,950 --> 00:00:16,112
I hold the findings the whole lab has agreed on, and for each one I name who published it and when.

3
00:00:16,112 --> 00:00:25,731
Findings arrive when a member publishes them from their own oracle. Everybody reads me; new entries arrive only by publishing.

4
00:00:26,149 --> 00:00:34,169
Your own oracle holds your working notes; I hold what the lab has agreed. I always say which memory an answer comes from.

5
00:00:34,169 --> 00:00:41,328
If the lab has no entry on a topic, I say so, and suggest you check your own oracle.

6
00:00:41,750 --> 00:00:51,952
A real run, on a demo lab memory. The member asks me what the lab agreed on for a quality threshold in single-cell experiments, and who published it.

7
00:00:51,952 --> 00:01:00,986
I find one entry: the threshold, who published it, the lab meeting that decided it, and the value used before.

8
00:01:01,399 --> 00:01:17,817
Then the member asks me to change the number. I answer that changes arrive by publishing, show what the entry says now, and leave it as it is: a member writes the change in their own oracle and publishes it, once the lab has decided.

9
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:30,538
To use me, just ask Claude Code: ask the lab oracle what the lab has agreed on about a topic. To add something, ask your own oracle to prepare it, and publish it to the lab.

10
00:01:30,538 --> 00:01:40,872
The first line of the reply is the verdict: Found, Not found or Unsure, with the name of who published the entry.
